Place executable system hooks under <event>/ directories.
User hooks under ~/.config/shinobi/hooks take effect for that user only.

Only executables sitting directly in <root>/<event>/ are ever run. Anything
nested deeper is ignored, by `run` and by `validate` alike.

## Event names

An event name becomes a path component, so it must match `[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*`
and be at most 64 characters. `shinobi hook install ../../cron.d/x ./payload`
is rejected rather than writing an executable outside the hooks tree.

## Roots must not be writable by anyone else

`shinobi hook run` executes whatever executables it finds in a hooks
directory, so a group- or world-writable root means any local user can plant a
hook that the next run will execute. Both roots are checked and a writable or
symlinked one is refused.

## User hooks ask first

A system hook under `/etc` is root-owned and runs without prompting: the live
image and scripts call `hook run` with no terminal, and gating those would
break the ISO for no security gain.

A user hook is code the current user planted, so it is confirmed first. With no
terminal to ask on, it is refused and the run reports what it skipped and
exits non-zero. To accept a hook you have read:

    shinobi hook run <event> --yes
    SHINOBI_HOOK_ASSUME_YES=1 shinobi hook run <event>

## install checks the script

`shinobi hook install` refuses a source with no shebang, a script that fails
`bash -n`, or a symlink, so a broken or redirected hook is not installed in the
first place. It prints a reminder that the result is executable code.
